<section class="section" id="fansociety">
	<div class="wrap">
		<span class="numeral" aria-hidden="true">05</span>

		<p class="eyebrow">FanSociety</p>
		<h2>Musician patronage, no subscriptions</h2>

		<div class="intro">
			<p class="lede">
				Fan clubs have been tried as rarity drops — ten thousand slots, first come, whoever has the
				most money. That sorts your supporters by wallet size and tells you nothing about them.
			</p>
			<p>
				FanSociety uses <strong>generations</strong> instead. You mint a generation and set what it
				costs. Fans mint into it for as long as it's open. When you close it and start the next one,
				that cohort is sealed — permanently, publicly, on-chain. Generation 1 stays Generation 1 for
				the length of your career.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="split">
			<article class="side">
				<div class="side-head">
					<h3>For you</h3>
					<span class="tag">artist</span>
				</div>
				<ul>
					<li>A record of who showed up and when, that grows with your career instead of selling out in a weekend.</li>
					<li>Grant early releases, bonus tracks, or free streaming to any generation you choose — per album, or per track.</li>
					<li>Membership revenue lands in your contract. See every supporter, every amount, every transaction.</li>
					<li>No cap, no tiers you have to invent up front, no scarcity theatre.</li>
				</ul>
			</article>

			<article class="side">
				<div class="side-head">
					<h3>For them</h3>
					<span class="tag">fan</span>
				</div>
				<ul>
					<li>Proof they were early that nobody can revoke, backfill, or quietly reprice.</li>
					<li>Access that the proof actually opens — held per generation, not as a blanket pass.</li>
					<li>Money that goes to the artist, in seconds, with the path visible on a block explorer.</li>
					<li>Something to point at when the band gets big. Which is the whole reason people care.</li>
				</ul>
			</article>
		</div>

		<p class="margin-note">
			The social contract between a fan and an artist already exists. FanSociety just writes it
			down somewhere neither party can edit later.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	.intro {
		margin: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem) 0 clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
	}

	.intro p + p {
		margin-top: 1.1rem;
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	.intro strong {
		color: var(--mint);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.split {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
		gap: 1px;
		background: var(--rule);
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.side {
		background: var(--black);
		padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
	}

	.side-head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 1rem;
		padding-bottom: 1.1rem;
		margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--mint-rule);
	}

	h3 {
		color: var(--mint);
	}

	.tag {
		font-size: 0.6rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.18em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--gray-dim);
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem;
		transform: rotate(1deg);
	}

	ul {
		list-style: none;
	}

	li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: 1.4rem;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
	}

	li + li {
		margin-top: 0.9rem;
	}

	li::before {
		content: '';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		top: 0.62em;
		width: 0.6rem;
		height: 1px;
		background: var(--mint);
	}
</style>
